Source: herdr 0.8.2

  herdr src/detect/mod.rs             -> src/engine.rs
  herdr src/detect/manifest.rs        -> src/manifest.rs
  herdr src/detect/manifest/tests.rs  -> src/manifest/tests.rs
  herdr src/detect/manifests/*.toml   -> src/manifests/*.toml  (21 files)
  herdr src/pane/agent_detection.rs   -> src/screen_rules.rs

The 21 .toml manifests are the detection rule data, embedded with include_str!
by src/manifest.rs; they are part of the ported work, not configuration.

One item was lifted out of herdr src/detect/manifest_update.rs, which is
otherwise not ported, into src/manifest.rs: `ManifestVersion`.

Modifications: crate-path rewrites; removal of the Remote manifest source and
the remote-update module.

The platform-specific process-identification block was removed wholesale in the
first port because it needed herdr's own `crate::platform` FFI layer, which R1
puts out of reach. Round 2 brought back the pure-string part of it, now that
Aleph has a foreground-process probe to feed it:

  herdr `identify_agent_in_job`      -> engine::identify_agent_from_process
        (src/detect/mod.rs:243-271)     (its single-process early return only;
                                         `process_priority` has no candidates
                                         to rank here and was not ported)
  herdr `normalized_process_name`    -> the name/argv0/cmdline ordering there
        (:359-395)
  herdr `is_generic_runtime_or_shell`-> engine::is_generic_runtime_or_shell
        (:696-711)                        (verbatim)
  herdr `is_python_runtime` (:713)   -> engine::is_python_runtime (verbatim)

Still not ported from that block: the runtime-specific argv walkers
(`cmd /c`, PowerShell `-File`, Cursor's bundled-node layout, the Qwen/bun
special cases) — each exists for a shape Aleph has no producer for.

src/screen_rules.rs keeps only `detection_update_for_publish_with_osc` of the
ten public items in herdr src/pane/agent_detection.rs; the rest were cut
because they are driven by upstream's poll interval and content-sequence
counter, which Aleph does not have. That file's header lists them by name.

The rest of the Aleph project is MIT-licensed. This crate is not. That split is
the reason this crate exists as a crate, so it is a boundary and not a note:
herdr-derived code goes in here, and nowhere else. `src/gateway/pty/foreground.rs`
in the main crate cites three of herdr's numbers (500 ms, 3 s, 6) in comments
and reproduces none of its code — see that file's header.
